## 7. Five-gate progression to blueprint

**G0 — Repository and CI baseline:** Confirm `moonwitness-xiv/dev`, baseline SHA, no concurrent issue writer, security scanner disposition, CI statuses. Do not promote `main` with failed required gates.

**G1 — Source/archive freeze:** Preserve V10 + V1–V9 snapshots in an authorized private location; public derivative redacted and reviewed; collect exact source locators/versions for each known family; mark missing items as `UNKNOWN`, not fabricated.

**G2 — Provenance and editorial review:** Rights/consent/privacy, source edition/translation, observed/occurred/recorded/created/published date distinctions, correction chain, version conflicts, source-to-artifact links. Core work maps to K5-002/K5-003.

**G3 — Correlation and canon candidates:** Reuse existing ontology contracts. Build narrative/semantic/research/capability relationships as *typed candidates*. Require explicit reviewer for identity equivalence, canon adoption and public projection. Maps to K5-004/K5-005.

**G4 — Capability & Blueprint:** Audit features and owner names already implemented across `apps/`, `packages/`, `ontology/`, `corpus/`, then produce an **experience blueprint** and an **architecture/implementation blueprint** without creating a duplicate API/graph store, changing runtime contracts, or inferring features from fiction. Tie every new backlog item to stable existing M/K IDs, dependencies and acceptance. Only then consider detailed UI, rollout and production gates.

## 8. Critical exclusions and sign-off

This handoff **does not**: approve public publication of V10; import personal chats; make original/AI-derived text canonical; merge identities on sound/name similarity; grant religious authority; approve a new runtime dependency; declare CI green; close an issue; authorize application deployment; or authorize an automatic `dev`→`main` release.

**Next concrete action:** Following security/CI stabilization, prepare a read-only source crosswalk and candidate matrix for `dev` while preserving all original material privately. Submit scoped changes through the established governance process and verify source SHA after writing. Only a human editorial owner may ratify canon or approve publication.

